          {
            "x": 2.178649237472767,
            "y": 12.910611231371064,
            "width": 92.73743016759778,
            "height": 86.09271523178808,
            "action": {
              "library": "H5P.AdvancedText 1.1",
              "params": {
                "text": "<p><strong>Influence and Praise</strong><br>\nBalzac's novels won him the admiration of many contemporaries and later writers. He was praised for his insights into society and the human condition, influencing authors like Charles Dickens, Marcel Proust, and Henry James.</p>\n\n<p><strong>Romantic Relationships</strong><br>\nBalzac's personal life was marked by a series of romantic entanglements, most notably with Ewelina Hańska, a Polish noblewoman with whom he corresponded for years before they eventually married in 1850.</p>\n\n<p><strong>Death and Legacy</strong><br>\nBalzac died five months after his wedding in 1850. He left behind a rich legacy as one of France's greatest novelists, with \"The Human Comedy\" remaining influential for its scope, characterization, and psychological depth.</p>\n"
              },
              "metadata": {
                "contentType": "Text",
                "license": "U",
                "title": "Unbenannt: Text",
                "defaultLanguage": "en"
              },
